# Windows scripts are stored with CRLF, byte for byte, and git is told never
# to touch them.
#
# Reported from a clinic, and it is the worst shape a bug can take: the update
# ran, succeeded, replaced the updater with a copy Windows could not run, and
# left the machine with no way to update again. `update.bat` opened and shut.
#
# cmd.exe reads a batch file expecting CRLF. With LF alone it mangles labels,
# `goto` and `^` line continuations — and the evidence was in which files broke
# and which did not: `start.bat` has none of the three and still ran, while
# `update.bat` has six labels, two gotos and eleven continuations and did not.
# `tools.bat` has fourteen labels, which is how a clinic loses the screen that
# restores a backup.
#
# `-text` rather than `eol=crlf` deliberately. `eol=crlf` fixes a checkout and
# does nothing for a ZIP downloaded from the web page — which is the copy most
# clinics have, and the copy this whole failure happened on. `-text` means the
# bytes in the repository are the bytes everybody gets, by every route.
*.bat -text
*.cmd -text
*.ps1 -text
